     "lbl": "Seizure",
     "type": "CLASS",
     "meta": {
      "definition": {
       "val": "A seizure is an intermittent abnormality of nervous system physiology characterized by a transient occurrence of signs and/or symptoms due to abnormal excessive or synchronous neuronal activity in the brain.",
       "xrefs": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199",
        "PMID:15816939"
       ]
      },
      "comments": [
       "A type of electrographic seizure has been proposed in neonates which does not have a clinical correlate, it is electrographic only. The term epilepsy is not used to describe recurrent febrile seizures. Epilepsy presumably reflects an abnormally reduced seizure threshold."
